{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1782560801,"nonce":"3TlZlWZyKC6o3qJAV2e7ehZT+mA=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHOK4fCvM8AAAABG5p-O87JGXuK"},"target":"be04f95c769a71236ef5bd920d2b660565655ec69d1fd755c7ec90419dd316f1","message":"## Motivation\n\nThe FCU 3D-gyro fault (#337) corrupted the FCU's attitude estimate — on June 26 `mavros/imu/data` showed phantom roll +2.5°→+23.6° while the independent **SBG INS held the true hull roll steady at +4.4°** (the two agreed to 0.2° on the healthy June 18). Because `mru_transform` sources orientation from `mavros/imu/data`, that phantom roll propagates into the `base_link` TF and gets painted into the bathymetry by `detections_to_pointcloud` — the roll artifacts seen in recent surveys.\n\nThe SBG stayed rock-solid through the gyro fault. **Make the SBG the primary navigation source and the FCU the backup**, so a degraded FCU can no longer poison bathy *or* nav attitude.\n\n## Current state\n\n`bizzyboat.yaml` (`/**/mru_transform`):\n```yaml\nsensors:\n  mru:\n    topics:\n      orientation: \"mavros/imu/data\"\n      position:    \"mavros/global_position/global\"\n      velocity:    \"mavros/local_position/velocity_body\"\nsensor_names: [mru]\n```\nSingle source, no failover.\n\n## mru_transform already supports priority + timeout failover\n\n`NavigationSensors::updateLatest` (navigation_sensors.hpp) scans `sensor_names` **in priority order** and adopts the highest-priority source whose latest sample is fresher than `sensor_timeout`, falling through to the next when one goes stale. It also publishes `nav/active_sensor/\u003cstream\u003e` so the live source is observable. So the mechanism is: order `[sbg, mru]` and set a sensible `sensor_timeout`.\n\n**Nuance:** arbitration is \"newest fresh sample wins, priority breaks ties,\" not strict primary-preference. With *both* sources healthy and at similar rates it can interleave SBG and FCU samples (which have a fixed ~1.9° attitude bias between them). Decide whether we need strict \"primary while fresh, backup only on dropout\" semantics (may need a small change), or whether running SBG at a higher rate so it's always newest is acceptable.\n\n## ✅ The two blockers are resolved — the stock driver already does what we need\n\nVerified against `sbg_driver` v3.3.2 source + a June 26 bag. **No custom converter/shim is needed.** The driver, with `ros_standard: true` (already set) **and `use_enu: true`**, publishes standard ROS messages that are drop-in compatible with `mru_transform`:\n\n- `sensors/imu/data` → `sensor_msgs/Imu`, **ENU** (REP-103) — orientation\n- `sensors/imu/nav_sat_fix` → `sensor_msgs/NavSatFix`, **ellipsoidal** — position\n- `sensors/imu/velocity` → `geometry_msgs/TwistStamped` — velocity\n\n### Why the standard topics aren't appearing today\n`ros_standard: true` is already set, but the driver **gates all standard publishers on `use_enu`**. From `message_publisher.cpp` `defineRosStandardPublishers()`:\n```cpp\nif (!enu_enable) {\n  RCLCPP_WARN(... \"Driver is configured in NED frame convention, ROS standard message are disabled.\");\n  return;   // no standard publishers created\n}\n```\nThe boat config has `use_enu: false`, so none of the standard topics are published. **The single enabling change is `use_enu: true`** in `sbg_ellipse_d.yaml`. This is a driver-side output convention — **not** a device-flash setting — so no `confWithRos`, no sbgCenter, no PORT_A rewire.\n\n### Altitude is already correct (no shim) — datum verified\nThe driver builds NavSatFix altitude as `altitude + undulation`. SBG `altitude` is MSL and `undulation` is the geoid–ellipsoid separation, so this yields **ellipsoidal** height. Verified on a June 26 bag at Lake Massabesic:\n\n`81.688 (SBG MSL) + (−32.785) (undulation) = 48.90 m` ≈ **48.75 m** (FCU `global_position/global`, ellipsoidal) ≈ `map_tide` z ≈ 48.8 m. ✓\n\nSo `sensors/imu/nav_sat_fix` is already on the pipeline's ellipsoidal datum — the previously-feared MSL-vs-ellipsoid shift does not occur when the SBG standard topic is used (it only would if a *native* `ekf_nav`/`gps_pos` MSL altitude were fed in raw).\n\n## Proposed approach\n\n1. **Enable SBG standard output:** set `use_enu: true` in `sbg_ellipse_d.yaml` (driver param; `confWithRos` stays false). Confirm `sensors/imu/{data,nav_sat_fix,velocity}` publish.\n2. **Add SBG as primary in `mru_transform`:**\n   ```yaml\n   sensors:\n     sbg:\n       topics:\n         orientation: \"sensors/imu/data\"\n         position:    \"sensors/imu/nav_sat_fix\"\n         velocity:    \"sensors/imu/velocity\"\n     mru:   # FCU fallback\n       topics:\n         orientation: \"mavros/imu/data\"\n         position:    \"mavros/global_position/global\"\n         velocity:    \"mavros/local_position/velocity_body\"\n   sensor_names: [sbg, mru]\n   ```\n   (FCU position is also ellipsoidal, so failover is datum-safe in both directions.)\n3. Add `sensors/imu/{data,nav_sat_fix,velocity}` to the boat `RECORD_TOPICS`.\n\n## Caveats to check before flipping `use_enu`\n\n1. **It also reorients the *native* `sbg/*` topics** (NED→ENU sign flips on `sbg/imu_data`, `ekf_quat`, …). Confirm nothing live consumes them in NED. QINSy reads the device serial directly — which `use_enu` does **not** affect — so QINSy is unaffected; still worth a sanity check.\n2. **`frame_id: \"imu_link_ned\"`** should become an ENU frame; standing TODO to add an SBG link to the URDF/TF (`config` comment).\n3. Confirm the arbitration nuance above is acceptable, or tighten to strict primary-preference.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- With SBG primary, replaying a June 26 bag: the `base_link` attitude TF follows the SBG (steady ~4.4°), **not** the FCU phantom roll; re-projected bathy shows no roll artifact.\n- Failover verified: SBG dropout → FCU takes over with **no** attitude discontinuity and **no** vertical jump (both ellipsoidal).\n- `nav/active_sensor/*` reflects the live source.\n- Vertical datum unchanged vs today (ellipsoidal; `map_tide` z ≈ 48.8 m) — confirmed on a bag.\n\n## Related\n\n- #337 — FCU 3D-gyro RCA (the fault this hardens against).\n- #338 — time-sync diagnostics (the M3 +6 s, a separate fault from the same investigation).\n- #76 — M3/SBG/SVS payload integration on mercat (SBG availability/host).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":5,"timestamp":1782558846,"nonce":"G9EOaqSPoCixyzHLwagRT7LNSPA=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOK4fCvM8AAAABG5p-O88AAAAGWe0i4A"},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1782560826,"nonce":"K5LqGfZpJSgVwL5Bwg6rEt8RXa8=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABHyF-ew","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/339#issuecomment-4817256059"},"message":"**Update — both blockers resolved; approach simplified** (body edited).\n\nDug into the SBG config (`sbg_ellipse_d.yaml`) and `sbg_driver` v3.3.2 source:\n\n- `ros_standard: true` is already set, but the driver **gates all standard publishers on `use_enu`** (`message_publisher.cpp`: `if (!enu_enable) { ...return; }`). The boat has `use_enu: false`, so the standard `sensors/imu/*` topics are never published — that's why bags only carry the native `sbg/*` messages. **Single enabling change: `use_enu: true`** (a driver-side output convention; no device-flash write, no PORT_A rewire).\n- The standard NavSatFix is built as `altitude + undulation` = **ellipsoidal**. Verified on a June 26 bag: `81.688 + (−32.785) = 48.90 m` ≈ the FCU's 48.75 m ≈ `map_tide` ≈ 48.8 m. So `sensors/imu/nav_sat_fix` is already on the pipeline's datum — **no MSL→ellipsoid shim needed**, and the earlier Phase-2 altitude hazard disappears.\n\nNet: this becomes \"flip `use_enu`, point a new `sbg` source in `mru_transform` at `sensors/imu/{data,nav_sat_fix,velocity}`, rank `[sbg, mru]`\" — no converter nodes. Remaining caveats (native `sbg/*` NED→ENU reorientation, `frame_id`/URDF SBG link, `RECORD_TOPICS`) are in the updated body.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":3,"timestamp":1782562191,"nonce":"Jbpl8OaKgoGMMrQJSexBwNAPVUw=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABHyM7sg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/339#issuecomment-4817370034"},"message":"**Position reference-point caveat — confirm the SBG GNSS lever arm before trusting bathy vertical.**\n\nThe standard `sensors/imu/nav_sat_fix` is built by the driver from `SbgGpsPos`, i.e. it reports position at the **GPS antenna phase center**, not the IMU/vehicle reference. Time-synced bag check confirms the offset is real and ~the configured lever arm:\n\n- `sbg/gps_pos` (antenna) − `sbg/ekf_nav` (IMU) = **+0.857 m** ≈ `gnss.primaryLeverArmZ` (0.881 m).\n- Cross-system sanity: the two antenna-referenced solutions (FCU `raw/fix` vs SBG `gps_pos`) agree to **4 cm**; the two body-referenced solutions (FCU `global` vs SBG `ekf_nav`) agree to **2.4 cm** — all consistent, RTK-level, once reference points match.\n\nImplication: when `mru_transform` takes `imu/nav_sat_fix` as the position source, the **SBG→base_link TF lever arm must be correct (~0.88 m antenna height)** or the bathy vertical shifts by that amount. This is the same \"add the SBG URDF link / fix `frame_id`\" TODO already flagged — calling it out explicitly because it directly moves the soundings in z. (Alternatively, source position from a body-referenced topic; but the standard NavSatFix is antenna-based.)\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}